    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d663">

                <pb facs="0721" n="613" sameAs="frus:frus1927v02/pg613"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.5112 France/13: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Herrick</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-05-27T13:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 27, 1927—1
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received May 27—11 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>217. At his lunch for Lindbergh<anchor n="3" xml:id="d663-f1"/> yesterday, Briand
                    told me that he wished in a few days to talk with me about his suggested pact
                    between France and the United States. He said that the reception of the idea was
                    so favorable that it ought to be studied, but that he did not desire to move
                    unless he was in entire agreement with President Coolidge.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Herrick</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d663-f1" n="3" xml:id="d663-f1-content">
                <p>Charles A. Lindbergh arrived in Paris May 21 on nonstop flight from New York.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
